test_dhello — Phase 1 chunk 9 dynamic-linker end-to-end
========================================================

Drives the chunk-9 PT_INTERP path through `PM_CMD_EXECVE`: this
program calls `pm_execve("/bin/dhello", ["dhello"])`, which on
success replaces the current process's image with the dynamically-
linked `dhello` binary. PM detects the PT_INTERP segment, loads
`/lib/ld-musl-aarch64.so.1` (or x86_64 sibling) at `INTERP_BASE`,
builds the rich auxv, and transfers control to ld-musl. ld-musl
runs its bootstrap, applies relocations, then calls `_start` in
the main exe which lands in `dhello`'s `main()` and prints
`hello dyn\n`.

A successful run produces exactly that line; any failure (file
not found, ELF malformed, ld-musl fails to bootstrap, etc.) falls
through to this program's tail and prints `dhello: bad <step>`.

What this test would fail on if regressed:

  * `pm_find_pt_interp` returning -1 when PT_INTERP is present →
    PM treats dhello as a static binary, doesn't load ld-musl, and
    the saved PC jumps to dhello's entry which doesn't have libc
    relocations applied.
  * `MAIN_EXE_BASE` rebasing not applied to a PIE main exe → the
    PT_LOAD VMAs land at link-time vaddrs (near 0) which fall
    outside the user-accessible carve-out [0x60000000, 0x61000000).
  * `INTERP_BASE` colliding with main exe or stack regions →
    overlapping VMAs or kernel-attribute pages, fault on first
    fetch in ld-musl.
  * Auxv missing AT_BASE / AT_PHDR / AT_ENTRY → ld-musl can't
    find its own program headers or doesn't know where to jump
    after bootstrap, exits early without running main.
  * `load_elf64_to_ptbr_at_offset` not adding `load_offset` to
    p_vaddr → main exe and interp content land at the wrong VAs
    despite VMAs being correctly placed.

    module oskit.bin.test_dhello

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, pm_execve}


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("dhello: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


Build the argv as a single-element array and call `pm_execve`. argv[0]
is the program name per SysV convention; ld-musl reads it for its own
diagnostics (LD_DEBUG) but `dhello`'s `main()` doesn't introspect argv.

    main(argv: []string)
        var args: [1]string
        args[0] = "dhello"
        val rc = pm_execve("/bin/dhello", &args[0], 1)

Reaching here means execve returned with a non-success code — the
binary was rejected before the point of no return.

        if rc < 0
            fail(1)
            return

Or execve nominally succeeded but the new program let control fall
through to this code instead of running its own _start, which would
indicate a frame-rewrite regression.

        fail(2)
